 the function to extract images, then iterate through them to see if 'iPad Mini' is in the alt text. I'll return a boolean or the image if found. Since the task is to 'check if it contains', I'll return a boolean. I'll plan: 1. On current page, find and click the article link. 2. Navigate to article page. 3. Extract images using 'extract_images_from_page_manual'. 4. Check if any image alt contains 'iPad Mini'. 5. Return result. I need to be careful with navigation: after clicking the article link, I should wait for the page to load. Then call the function. I'll use relative URLs where possible. Since I don't know the article URL, I'll find it dynamically.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Step 1: Find the article link on the current page\n    try:\n        # Look for articles on the page\n        articles = page.get_by_role('article')\n        count = await articles.count()\n        if count == 0:\n            # If no articles, maybe they are in links or other roles\n            # Check for links that might contain the article title\n            all_links = page.get_by_role('link')\n            link_count = await all_links.count()\n            for i in range(link_count):\n                link = all_links.nth(i)\n                text = await link.text_content()\n                if text and 'Ruby 4.0 update brings ZJIT, isolation features, and more' in text:\n                    await link.click()\n                    await page.wait_for_load_state('load')\n                    break\n            else:\n                # If still not found, assume the article is at a specific URL\n                # Use a relative URL guess based on common patterns\n                await page.goto('/?p=ruby-4-0-update', wait_until='load')\n        else:\n            # Filter articles to find the one with the correct name\n            target_article = articles.filter(has=page.get_by_text('Ruby 4.0 update brings ZJIT, isolation features, and more', exact=False))\n      